  "query_preview": "../Sunday through Wednesday/...\nMinimal changes were made to the long term forecast as mild \nweather continues to end out the month of October. High pressure \nremains over the gulf through the long term period, with brief \ninterruptions by weak low pressure systems. The first of these \nwill move across the gulf Monday bringing increased cloud cover \nthe the chance for light precipitation. The main change with this \nweak front is that the timing looks to have slightly changed with \nit arriving slightly later than initially anticipated. Now \narriving Sunday night into Monday. Currently no impacts are \nanticipated with just light precipitation expected. The other main\nchange to the long term forecast was increasing winds Tuesday as \nthe northerly winds are once again likely to increase as the \npressure gradient builds. Currently strongest winds are most \nlikely to occur over Lynn Canal into Skagway. \n\nThere is a chance for some northern areas to see slightly below \naverage temperatures into next week into the high 20s / low 30s. \nThis will be mainly dependent on how clear we get at night, with \nlow cloud cover. Otherwise temperatures remain around typical for \nthis time of year with maximum temperatures in the 40s and low \ntemperatures in the low to mid 30s.",
